Looking at the code fragments, I can see that `hexi::file_buffer` has `read()` and `write()` template methods that can be used to read and write primitive types directly. Based on the test examples, the buffer is opened with a file path and provides methods to read/write data.

Here's the implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/file_buffer.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <filesystem>

uint32_t read_uint32_from_file(const std::filesystem::path& file_path) {
    hexi::file_buffer buffer(file_path);
    if (!buffer) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open file for reading");
    }

    uint32_t value = 0;
    buffer.read(&value);

    if (!buffer) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to read uint32 from file");
    }

    return value;
}

void write_uint32_to_file(const std::filesystem::path& file_path, uint32_t value) {
    hexi::file_buffer buffer(file_path);
    if (!buffer) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open file for writing");
    }

    buffer.write(value);
    buffer.flush();

    if (!buffer) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to write uint32 to file");
    }
}
```

